  "problemStatement": {
    "formal": "\\text{In } \\mathbb{R}^n:\\ \\{x : \\exists i,\\ x_i \\in \\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}\\} \\text{ is Lebesgue-null, and } \\overline{\\mathbb{Q}}^n \\text{ is countable (hence null).}",
    "plain": "Lift the parent's 1-dimensional result (the algebraic reals are countable, hence Lebesgue-null) to R^n and C^n: the set of points with at least one algebraic coordinate is null, and the fully-algebraic points are countable. Almost every point has all coordinates transcendental and algebraically independent.",
